---
{state.status === "retired" ? <StateEvent view={state} /> : <BaseLayout title={`${event.title} | The Catch`} current="Events">
  <div slot="locator" class="locator"><a href="/events/">Series</a><a href="/events/government-funding/">Government funding</a><span>September 2026</span></div>
  <div class="content">
    <main class="story" id="story-root">
      <p class="story-kicker" data-layer="narrative">U.S. Congress &middot; September 1, 2026</p>
      <h1 data-layer="fact">{event.title}</h1>
      <p class="story-dek" data-layer="fact">{event.dek}</p>
      <ReadingModes />

      <nav class="story-toc" aria-label="In this story" data-layer="fact">
        <details class="toc-disclosure" open><summary><span>In this story</span><span class="toc-current" aria-hidden="true"></span></summary>
        <ol>
          <li><a href="#what-happened">What happened</a></li>
          <li><a href="#the-catch">The catch</a></li>
          <li><a href="#where-this-sits">Where this sits</a></li>
          <li><a href="#who-feels-it">Who feels it</a></li>
          <li><a href="#outlets">What the coverage got right, and what it got wrong</a></li>
          <li><a href="#claim-check">Claim checked against the record</a></li>
          <li><a href="#markets">What the markets said</a></li>
          <li><a href="#what-happened-next">What happened next</a></li>
          <li><a href="#unknowns">What we do not know yet</a></li>
          <li><a href="#records">The records</a></li>
        </ol>
      </details></nav>
      <div class="kpi-strip" data-layer="fact">
        {event.kpis.map((k) => (
          <div class="kpi">
            <span class="kpi-value">{k.value}<small>{k.unit ? ` ${k.unit}` : ""}</small></span>
            <span class="kpi-label">{k.label}</span>
          </div>
        ))}
      </div>

      <aside class="three-things" data-layer="fact">
        <p class="tt-label">Three things to know</p>
        <ol>
          <li>On September 1 the House voted {houseVote.yeas} to {houseVote.nays} to suspend the rules and concur in the Senate's amendments to H.R. 6500, the fast-track path that allows no amendments and needs two-thirds. Republicans voted {houseVote.republican.yea} to {houseVote.republican.nay}. Democrats voted {houseVote.democratic.yea} to {houseVote.democratic.nay}. Independent Kiley of California voted yes. The House had 432 members that day; 14 did not vote.<Cite s="house-clerk-roll286" passage="On Motion to Suspend the Rules and Concur in the Senate Amendments" /><Cite s="crec-2026-09-01" passage="the whole number of the House is 432" /></li>
          <li>The enrolled bill funds most agencies at fiscal year 2026 rates through December 11, 2026, or until a full-year 2027 appropriations act is signed, whichever comes first. That date is {calendar.daysHouseVoteToExpiry} days after the House vote and {calendar.daysElectionToExpiry} days after the November 3 federal election.<Cite s="hr6500-enrolled" passage="December 11, 2026" /><Cite s="fec-election-dates" passage="Tuesday, November 3, 2026 , is the next regularly scheduled federal General Election date" /></li>
          <li>The Senate passed the same text {senateVote.yeas} to {senateVote.nays} on August 8. The president signed it September 2 as Public Law 119-103.<Cite s="senate-vote-228" passage="Bill Passed (90-6, 3/5 majority required)" /><Cite s="archives-laws-current" passage="119-103" /></li>
        </ol>
      </aside>
      <p class="railed rail-note" data-layer="fact">Story updated {event.updated}.</p>

      <div data-layer="proof" class="event-record-context">
        <p class="sources-line">This story rests on the enrolled text of H.R. 6500, the House Clerk's roll calls, the Senate roll calls, Public Law 119-37, Public Law 119-86, the National Archives public-law list, the White House signing note, the Office of Management and Budget's August 3 policy statement, the Congressional Record, floor and press statements from the Speaker and the appropriations chairs, and coverage checked against those records. The Congressional Budget Office's site did not let its pages be saved from this machine on September 8. Axios and Politico copies were saved September 8 with a browser-imitating fetch.</p>
      </div>

      <section id="what-happened">
        <p class="sec-kicker" data-layer="narrative">The vote</p>
        <h2 data-layer="fact">What happened</h2>

        <SourcedBlock source="house-clerk-roll286" kind="record">
          <p>On September 1, 2026, at 2:03 p.m., the House voted on a motion to suspend the rules and concur in the Senate amendments to H.R. 6500. Suspend the rules is the fast-track path: it allows no amendments and needs two-thirds.<Cite s="house-clerk-roll286" passage="On Motion to Suspend the Rules and Concur in the Senate Amendments" /></p></SourcedBlock>

        <SourcedBlock source="house-clerk-roll286" kind="record">
          <p>The Clerk recorded the result as passed, {houseVote.yeas} yeas to {houseVote.nays} nays.<Cite s="house-clerk-roll286" passage="Passed" /></p></SourcedBlock>

        <SourcedBlock source="house-clerk-roll286" kind="record">
          <p>The Clerk's description field on that roll call still reads "AGOA Extension Act."<Cite s="house-clerk-roll286" passage="AGOA Extension Act" /></p></SourcedBlock>

        <SourcedBlock source="hr6500-enrolled" kind="record">
          <p>The enrolled act's short title is the Continuing Appropriations and Extensions Act, 2027.<Cite s="hr6500-enrolled" passage="This Act may be cited as the" /></p></SourcedBlock>

        <SourcedBlock source="hr6500-enrolled" kind="record">
          <p>Section 106 says the money lasts until a 2027 appropriations act is signed, or until December 11, 2026, whichever comes first. Section 101 sets the rate as the fiscal year 2026 appropriations acts named there, with listed exceptions.<Cite s="hr6500-enrolled" passage="December 11, 2026" /></p></SourcedBlock>

        <p data-layer="narrative">A continuing resolution is a stopgap: it keeps agencies running at last year's rate while Congress has not finished the twelve full-year spending bills. Fiscal year 2027 begins October 1. If no new act were signed by September 30, the 2026 appropriations named in section 101 would have lapsed.<Cite s="hr6500-enrolled" passage="Such amounts as may be necessary, at a rate for" /><Cite s="omb-sap-hr6500" passage="current levels" /></p>

        <SourcedBlock source="senate-vote-228" kind="record">
          <p>The Senate passed H.R. 6500, as amended, {senateVote.yeas} to {senateVote.nays}.<Cite s="senate-vote-228" passage="Bill Passed (90-6, 3/5 majority required)" /></p></SourcedBlock>

        <SourcedBlock source="senate-vote-228-time" kind="record">
          <p>That vote was recorded at 3:37 a.m. on August 8, 2026.<Cite s="senate-vote-228-time" passage="August 8, 2026,  03:37 AM" /></p></SourcedBlock>

        <SourcedBlock source="senate-vote-228" kind="record">
          <p>The Senate's own title on that vote is "An act making continuing appropriations and extensions for fiscal year 2027, and for other purposes."<Cite s="senate-vote-228" passage="An act making continuing appropriations and extensions for fiscal year 2027, and for other purposes." /></p></SourcedBlock>

        <SourcedBlock source="senate-vote-228" kind="record">
          <p>The nays were Cassidy, Kaine, Markey, Paul, Sanders, and Warren. Graham voted present. Lankford, McConnell, and Tillis did not vote.<Cite s="senate-vote-228" passage="Cassidy" /></p></SourcedBlock>

        <SourcedBlock source="crec-2026-08-07" kind="record">
          <p>Those August 8 votes sit in the August 7 Congressional Record, the Senate's legislative day, which is why the session ran past 3 a.m. Earlier that sitting the Senate voted 91 to 6 to end debate on the Collins substitute amendment No. 6732.<Cite s="crec-2026-08-07" passage="By 91 yeas to 6 nays, one Senator responding" /><Cite s="senate-vote-228-time" passage="August 8, 2026,  03:37 AM" /></p></SourcedBlock>

        <SourcedBlock source="plaw-119-37" kind="record">
          <p>Public Law 119-37, approved November 12, 2025, rewrites what counts as legal hemp. Section 781 says: "Effective 365 days after the enactment of this Act, Section 297A of the Agricultural Marketing Act of 1946 (7 U.S.C. 1639o) is amended." That date is November 12, 2026. The new definition caps hemp at 0.3 percent "total tetrahydrocannabinols" and excludes "intermediate hemp-derived cannabinoid products" and "final hemp-derived cannabinoid products" that contain "cannabinoids that are not capable of being naturally produced by a Cannabis sativa L. plant."<Cite s="plaw-119-37" passage="SEC. 781. Effective 365 days after the enactment of this Act," /><Cite s="plaw-119-37" passage="Approved November 12, 2025" /></p></SourcedBlock>

        <SourcedBlock source="hr6500-enrolled" kind="record">
          <p>Enrolled section 2019, headed "SECTION 781 EXTENSION," does not use the word hemp, which is why a text search of the stopgap misses it. It says: "Until December 11, 2026, the amendments made by section 781 ... shall only apply with respect to products described in paragraphs (1)(C)(ii)(I) and (1)(C)(iv)(I)." Between November 12 and December 11 only the part aimed at products made with cannabinoids a cannabis plant cannot produce on its own (lab-made cannabinoids) applies. The 0.3 percent total-THC line and the other exclusions wait until December 11. That wait is {hemp.delayDays} days.<Cite s="hr6500-enrolled" passage="SEC. 2019. SECTION 781 EXTENSION." /><Cite s="hr6500-enrolled" passage="Until December 11, 2026, the amendments made by section" /></p></SourcedBlock>

        <p data-layer="narrative">Outlets that called this a delay of the hemp ban were right, with one omission most of them made: the lab-made-cannabinoid part is not delayed.<Cite s="thehill-house-funding" passage="short-term delay of a ban on intoxicating hemp products" /><Cite s="hr6500-enrolled" passage="SEC. 2019. SECTION 781 EXTENSION." /></p>

        <SourcedBlock source="senate-vote-227" kind="record">
          <p>An hour before passage, at 2:36 a.m., the Senate tabled an amendment by Senator Ted Budd, {buddVote.yeas} to {buddVote.nays}. The Senate's title on that vote is "Motion to Table Budd Amdt. No. 6747." Its stated purpose is "To strike section 2019."<Cite s="senate-vote-227" passage="Motion to Table Agreed to (61-32)" /><Cite s="senate-vote-227-purpose" passage="To strike section 2019." /></p></SourcedBlock>

        <SourcedBlock source="senate-vote-227" kind="record">
          <p>Republicans voted {buddVote.republican.yea} yea and {buddVote.republican.nay} nay. Democrats voted {buddVote.democratic.yea} yea and {buddVote.democratic.nay} nay. One independent voted yea.<Cite s="senate-vote-227" passage="Motion to Table Agreed to (61-32)" /></p></SourcedBlock>

        <SourcedBlock source="cole-floor-2026-09-01" kind="record">
          <p>House Appropriations Chairman Tom Cole, leading the floor debate, said the Senate-amended bill "gives the nation, and our constituents, certainty": that the government will remain open, that service members will be paid, that TSA will keep operating, and that Social Security checks will go out on time.<Cite s="cole-floor-2026-09-01" passage="certainty that the government will remain open" /></p></SourcedBlock>

        <SourcedBlock source="cole-house-passage-2026-09-01" kind="record">
          <p>The House Appropriations committee's written statement the same day said the measure was approved by a vote of 370-48 and would go to the president's desk.<Cite s="cole-house-passage-2026-09-01" passage="The measure was approved by a vote of 370" /></p></SourcedBlock>

        <SourcedBlock source="speaker-johnson-2026-09-01" kind="record">
          <p>After the vote, Speaker Mike Johnson said House Republicans had begun the work in July so the government would not shut down, and that troops, the Coast Guard, and TSA would not go unpaid. He credited "House Republicans' leadership and the foresight back in July."<Cite s="speaker-johnson-2026-09-01" passage="the foresight back in July" /></p></SourcedBlock>

        <SourcedBlock source="delauro-house-passage-2026-09-01" kind="record">
          <p>House Appropriations Ranking Member Rosa DeLauro issued a statement the same day: the deadline had been pushed to December 11, she remained committed to passing all twelve full-year bills before then, and "We have 101 days until government funding expires."<Cite s="delauro-house-passage-2026-09-01" passage="We have 101 days until government funding expires" /></p></SourcedBlock>
      </section>

      <section id="the-catch">
        <aside class="the-catch" data-layer="fact">
          <p class="tt-label">The catch</p>
          <ol>
            <li>
              <span class="catch-tag">Told versus record</span>
              <span class="catch-lead">Still labeled AGOA.</span>
              The Clerk's roll call 286 is titled "AGOA Extension Act," the name H.R. 6500 carried when the House passed it in January as a trade bill, 340 to 54. The enrolled act's title is the Continuing Appropriations and Extensions Act, 2027. The trade extension survived inside it: section 2008, "EXTENSION OF AFRICAN GROWTH AND OPPORTUNITY ACT," strikes "2026" and inserts "2028," and extends the regional apparel program "until December 31, 2028." The Clerk's label is stale as a title and not wrong about that cargo.<Cite s="house-clerk-roll286" passage="AGOA Extension Act" /><Cite s="house-clerk-roll014" passage="AGOA Extension Act" /><Cite s="hr6500-enrolled" passage="SEC. 2008. EXTENSION OF AFRICAN GROWTH AND OPPORTUNITY ACT." />
              <a class="catch-jump" href="#claim-check">Claim check</a>
            </li>
            <li>
              <span class="catch-tag">Told versus record</span>
              <span class="catch-lead">The July bill was not this bill.</span>
              Cole said "in July the House proactively passed H.R. 9770, a continuing resolution to fund the government through December 4 ... The Senate built on our plan." Johnson credited "the foresight back in July." The July bill passed 220 to 205, Republicans 213 to 1, Democrats 6 to 204, Independent 1 to 0, with a December 4 date. The Senate never voted on it. The Senate rewrote the January trade bill, H.R. 6500, and the House adopted that Senate text without change.<Cite s="cole-floor-2026-09-01" passage="in July the House proactively passed H.R. 9770" /><Cite s="house-clerk-roll272" passage="Making continuing appropriations for fiscal year 2027, and for other purposes" /><Cite s="speaker-johnson-2026-09-01" passage="the foresight back in July" />
              <a class="catch-jump" href="#where-this-sits">Where this sits</a>
            </li>
            <li>
              <span class="catch-tag">Left out</span>
              <span class="catch-lead">The hemp ban moved four weeks, mostly.</span>
              Several outlets said the Senate delayed a ban on intoxicating hemp products. The 2025 law narrows legal hemp starting November 12, 2026. Between that date and December 11 only the lab-made-cannabinoid part applies; the rest of the new definition waits {hemp.delayDays} days. The bill's own heading does not say hemp.<Cite s="thehill-house-funding" passage="short-term delay of a ban on intoxicating hemp products" /><Cite s="hr6500-enrolled" passage="SEC. 2019. SECTION 781 EXTENSION." /><Cite s="plaw-119-37" passage="SEC. 781. Effective 365 days after the enactment of this Act," />
              <a class="catch-jump" href="#what-happened">What happened</a>
            </li>
            <li>
              <span class="catch-tag">Who pays</span>
              <span class="catch-lead">{calendar.daysHouseVoteToExpiry} days, last year's rate.</span>
              The stopgap does not set a new annual total. It continues 2026 rates through December 11. WIC may be spent at the rate needed to maintain participation. Funded agencies keep paying their civilian staff, and the budget office may release pay money at the rate needed to avoid furloughs. The next lapse date is {calendar.daysElectionToExpiry} days after Election Day.<Cite s="hr6500-enrolled" passage="maintain participation" /><Cite s="hr6500-enrolled" passage="to avoid furloughs within such department or agency" />
              <a class="catch-jump" href="#who-feels-it">Who feels it</a>
            </li>
          </ol>
        </aside>
      </section>

      <section id="where-this-sits">
        <p class="sec-kicker" data-layer="narrative">How the stopgap got here</p>
        <h2 data-layer="fact">Where this sits</h2>

        <SourcedBlock source="cole-floor-2026-09-01" kind="record">
          <p>Cole told the House that in July it had passed H.R. 9770, a continuing resolution through December 4. The Senate, he said, built on that plan and passed an amended version before leaving for recess. The September 1 vote was to take up that Senate text.<Cite s="cole-floor-2026-09-01" passage="in July the House proactively passed H.R. 9770" /></p></SourcedBlock>

        <SourcedBlock source="house-clerk-roll272" kind="record">
          <p>House roll call 272 on July 21, 2026, is the July bill: "Making continuing appropriations for fiscal year 2027, and for other purposes," on passage, {julyVote.yeas} to {julyVote.nays}. Republicans voted {julyVote.republican.yea} to {julyVote.republican.nay}. Democrats voted {julyVote.democratic.yea} to {julyVote.democratic.nay}. Independent 1 to 0.<Cite s="house-clerk-roll272" passage="Making continuing appropriations for fiscal year 2027, and for other purposes" /></p></SourcedBlock>

        <SourcedBlock source="house-clerk-roll014" kind="record">
          <p>H.R. 6500 began as a January trade bill. On January 12 the House passed it, as amended, {januaryVote.yeas} to {januaryVote.nays} on a motion to suspend the rules, titled "AGOA Extension Act." Republicans voted {januaryVote.republican.yea} to {januaryVote.republican.nay}. Democrats voted {januaryVote.democratic.yea} to {januaryVote.democratic.nay}. AGOA is the African Growth and Opportunity Act, a trade-preference law for sub-Saharan African countries. Cole, on the September 1 floor, called it a "partnership with the nations of sub-Saharan Africa."<Cite s="house-clerk-roll014" passage="On Motion to Suspend the Rules and Pass, as Amended" /><Cite s="crec-2026-09-01" passage="nations of sub-Saha-" /></p></SourcedBlock>

        <p data-layer="narrative">The Senate used that bill number as the vehicle for the stopgap, changed the title, and sent the rewritten text back. The Clerk's record still prints the old name on roll call 286. The enrolled parchment carries the new one, and keeps a two-year AGOA extension inside it.<Cite s="house-clerk-roll286" passage="AGOA Extension Act" /><Cite s="hr6500-enrolled" passage="SEC. 2008. EXTENSION OF AFRICAN GROWTH AND OPPORTUNITY ACT." /></p>

        <SourcedBlock source="crec-2026-09-01" kind="record">
          <p>Cole also told the House that "all 12 bills were reported out of committee in the fastest time" since fiscal year 2020.<Cite s="crec-2026-09-01" passage="all 12 bills were reported out of" /></p></SourcedBlock>

        <SourcedBlock source="omb-sap-hr6500" kind="record">
          <p>On August 3, before the Senate vote, the Office of Management and Budget issued a Statement of Administration Policy supporting the Senate amendment. It called the bill a "clean, short-term continuing resolution" at current levels through December 11, and said the president's senior advisors would recommend he sign it.<Cite s="omb-sap-hr6500" passage="The Administration supports the Senate Amendment to H.R. 6500." /></p></SourcedBlock>

        <SourcedBlock source="fox-schumer-funding" kind="record">
          <p>Fox News, the same day, quoted Senate Minority Leader Chuck Schumer calling the Senate text the "responsible path forward that allows continued bipartisan negotiations on a budget that delivers for American families."<Cite s="fox-schumer-funding" passage="responsible path forward that allows continued bipartisan negotiations on a budget that delivers for American families" /></p></SourcedBlock>

        <SourcedBlock source="hr6500-enrolled" kind="record">
          <p>Section 157 bars, through December 11, a rule revising the Uniform Guidance for federal grants, the notice of proposed rulemaking titled "Regulation for Federal Financial Assistance" published May 29, 2026, or a substantially similar rule.<Cite s="hr6500-enrolled" passage="Uniform Guidance" /></p></SourcedBlock>

        <SourcedBlock source="fr-2026-10817" kind="record">
          <p>That May 29 Federal Register notice said "Comments are due on or before July 13, 2026" and "OMB proposes to issue a final rule that is effective by October 1, 2026." The rule OMB wanted in force October 1 cannot be issued or take effect through December 11.<Cite s="fr-2026-10817" passage="Comments are due on or before" /><Cite s="fr-2026-10817" passage="OMB proposes to issue a final rule" /><Cite s="hr6500-enrolled" passage="shall not be issued or" /></p></SourcedBlock>

        <SourcedBlock source="murray-senate-passage-2026-08-08" kind="record">
          <p>Senate Appropriations Vice Chair Patty Murray, on August 8, said the negotiated stopgap "closes the loophole that would have allowed the Trump administration to transfer funding for other programs to Border Patrol." That sentence is Murray's wording. The record behind it is real: enrolled section 101(6) continues Public Law 119-86 except division B. Division A of that 2026 Homeland Security law is the "Department of Homeland Security Appropriations Act, 2026." Its section 549 says no amounts may be transferred to Customs and Border Protection, Operations and Support, for Border Security Operations. The transfer bar continues with that division. Division B, the earlier stopgap attached to that law, is not carried forward.<Cite s="murray-senate-passage-2026-08-08" passage="It closes the loophole that would have allowed the Trump administration to transfer funding for other programs to Border Patrol." /><Cite s="plaw-119-86" passage="SEC. 549. Notwithstanding section 503(c) of this Act, no" /><Cite s="hr6500-enrolled" passage="division B, and including sections 5013 through 5016 of division" /></p></SourcedBlock>

        <p data-layer="narrative">NPR places this vote after two long funding lapses: "a 43-day shutdown that ended in November" and "A 76-day shutdown at the Department of Homeland Security came to a close in April." Those counts are NPR's. The law that ended the fall 2025 shutdown, Public Law 119-37, was approved November 12, 2025.<Cite s="npr-house-funding" passage="a 43-day shutdown that ended in November" /><Cite s="npr-house-funding" passage="A 76-day shutdown at the Department of Homeland Security came to a close in April" /><Cite s="plaw-119-37" passage="Approved November 12, 2025" /></p>

        <p data-layer="narrative">The 370-48 margin hides a fight over hemp and abortion funding. Andy Harris told Roll Call he would vote against a rule that came out without the continuing resolution and with a hemp amendment to undo what the Senate did. He told MS NOW: "The House and the Senate disagree on the continuing resolution. We should go to conference." Cole told Roll Call, "Hyde is the law of the land." CNBC reported that a Roy and Arrington letter objected to "funding for Planned Parenthood and the absence of a ban on hemp products that contain THC."<Cite s="rollcall-house-funding" passage="If the rule comes out without the CR in it" /><Cite s="msnow-house-funding" passage="The House and the Senate disagree on the continuing resolution" /><Cite s="rollcall-house-funding" passage="Hyde is the law of the land" /><Cite s="cnbc-house-funding" passage="funding for Planned Parenthood" /></p>

        <SourcedBlock source="guardian-house-funding" kind="record">
          <p>Before the House vote, the Guardian quoted Democratic Caucus Chair Pete Aguilar: "Given that the objectives that we had were met," he said, there would be "broad bipartisan support."<Cite s="guardian-house-funding" passage="Given that the objectives that we had were met" /><Cite s="guardian-house-funding" passage="broad bipartisan support" /></p></SourcedBlock>

        <SourcedBlock source="rollcall-house-funding" kind="record">
          <p>Roll Call wrote that "Tuesday's House vote marked the earliest completion in recent memory of a continuing resolution."<Cite s="rollcall-house-funding" passage="earliest completion in recent memory" /></p></SourcedBlock>

        <p data-layer="narrative">Axios reported that "President Trump personally intervened Tuesday night in a dispute over delaying a ban on hemp-derived THC products, calling a key senator"; that the provision "would delay the ban by about a month"; and that a delay "would reportedly benefit White House Chief of Staff Susie Wiles' son-in-law." Politico quoted Budd: "it is morally reprehensible to threaten a shutdown over a loophole Congress agreed to close to less than a year ago." Those sentences are the outlets'. No White House document in our records mentions hemp. The August 3 policy statement and the September 2 signing note do not.<Cite s="axios-hemp" passage="President Trump personally intervened Tuesday night" /><Cite s="politico-hemp" passage="it is morally reprehensible to threaten a shutdown" /><Cite s="omb-sap-hr6500" passage="The Administration supports the Senate Amendment to H.R. 6500." /></p>

        <details class="receipt" data-layer="proof"><summary>how the calendar was counted</summary>
          <p>House vote date from the Clerk: September 1, 2026. Senate vote date from the Senate XML: August 8, 2026. Signature date from the White House note: September 2, 2026. Expiry date from enrolled section 106(3): December 11, 2026. Election Day from the Federal Election Commission: November 3, 2026. Subtraction of those dates yields {calendar.daysSenateToHouse} elapsed days from the Senate vote to the House vote, {calendar.daysHouseVoteToExpiry} from the House vote to December 11, {calendar.daysHouseVoteToElection} from the House vote to November 3, and {calendar.daysElectionToExpiry} from November 3 to December 11. DeLauro's "101 days" matches the House-vote-to-expiry count. Hemp: 365 days after November 12, 2025 is November 12, 2026; November 12 to December 11 is {hemp.delayDays} elapsed days.</p>
        </details>

        <details class="receipt" data-layer="proof"><summary>who voted no on roll call 286</summary>
          <p>From house-roll286.xml member rows. Republican nays ({houseVote.republicanNays.length}): {houseVote.republicanNays.join(", ")}. Democratic nays ({houseVote.democraticNays.length}): {houseVote.democraticNays.join(", ")}.</p>
        </details>
      </section>

      <section id="who-feels-it">
        <p class="sec-kicker" data-layer="narrative">Workers, programs, and the next date</p>
        <h2 data-layer="fact">Who feels it</h2>

        <SourcedBlock source="cole-floor-2026-09-01" kind="record">
          <p>Cole's list of who keeps getting paid or served is the floor record: service members, TSA, Social Security recipients, homebuyers seeking mortgages, people on nutrition assistance, disaster relief, and road and infrastructure projects. Those are the programs he named. The enrolled bill itself names the twelve 2026 appropriations acts it continues.<Cite s="cole-floor-2026-09-01" passage="certainty that our servicemembers will be paid" /><Cite s="hr6500-enrolled" passage="The Agriculture, Rural Development, Food and Drug" /></p></SourcedBlock>

        <SourcedBlock source="hr6500-enrolled" kind="record">
          <p>One named exception to a straight freeze is WIC, the Special Supplemental Nutrition Program for Women, Infants, and Children. Amounts for WIC "may be apportioned at the rate for operations necessary to maintain participation." The Commodity Supplemental Food Program, which serves low-income seniors, may be spent at the rate needed to keep current caseload.<Cite s="hr6500-enrolled" passage="maintain participation" /><Cite s="hr6500-enrolled" passage="Commodity Supplemental Food Program" /></p></SourcedBlock>

        <SourcedBlock source="hr6500-enrolled" kind="record">
          <p>Section 112 says amounts for civilian personnel compensation and benefits "may be apportioned up to the rate for operations necessary to avoid furloughs." Funded agencies keep paying their civilian staff, and the budget office may release pay money at the rate needed to avoid furloughs.<Cite s="hr6500-enrolled" passage="to avoid furloughs within such department or agency" /></p></SourcedBlock>

        <p data-layer="narrative">The Office of Management and Budget's August 3 statement said a shutdown "forces Federal employees to go without pay." Some work continues in a lapse; that sentence is OMB's, about people going unpaid.<Cite s="omb-sap-hr6500" passage="forces Federal employees to go without pay" /></p>

        <SourcedBlock source="wh-hr6500-signed" kind="record">
          <p>The White House signing note also names surface transportation and veteran programs among the authorities the act extends. Those sit in the enrolled bill's later divisions, not in Cole's floor list.<Cite s="wh-hr6500-signed" passage="extends authorities for a broad range of programs including surface transportation and veteran programs" /></p></SourcedBlock>

        <SourcedBlock source="hr6500-enrolled" kind="record">
          <p>People who already receive federal grants keep working under today's rules. Section 157 only delays one proposed rewrite of the Uniform Guidance. Separately, section 104 bars using the money "to initiate or resume any project or activity" not funded in 2026; section 109 caps programs "that would otherwise have high initial rates of operation or complete distribution" and says "no grants shall be awarded for such programs funded by this Act that would impinge on final funding prerogatives"; section 110 requires "only the most limited funding action." No new programs, no front-loaded grant rounds, and the Uniform Guidance rewrite postponed. Collins, the Senate Appropriations chair, called that rule a plan that "would politicize the grant process."<Cite s="hr6500-enrolled" passage="to initiate or resume any project" /><Cite s="hr6500-enrolled" passage="no grants shall be awarded for such programs funded" /><Cite s="hr6500-enrolled" passage="most limited funding action of that permitted" /><Cite s="collins-house-passage-2026-09-01" passage="which would politicize the grant process" /></p></SourcedBlock>

        <SourcedBlock source="aashto-house-cr" kind="record">
          <p>The American Association of State Highway and Transportation Officials said the road and transit authorities continue, but because the stopgap "does not provide funding for the Division J advance appropriations programs contained within the Infrastructure Investment and Jobs Act or IIJA," no 2027 money would be available October 1 for named rail, bridge, and charging programs, including "the Federal-State Partnership for Intercity Passenger Rail grant program" and "the Bridge Formula Program."<Cite s="aashto-house-cr" passage="Division J advance appropriations programs contained within the Infrastructure Investment and Jobs Act" /></p></SourcedBlock>

        <p data-layer="narrative">The December 11 deadline falls {calendar.daysElectionToExpiry} days after the November 3 federal election. DeLauro's statement treats those {calendar.daysHouseVoteToExpiry} days as the time left to finish the twelve full-year bills.<Cite s="fec-election-dates" passage="Tuesday, November 3, 2026 , is the next regularly scheduled federal General Election date" /><Cite s="delauro-house-passage-2026-09-01" passage="passing all twelve full-year funding bills before the expiration of this resolution" /></p>
      </section>

      <section id="outlets">
        <p class="sec-kicker" data-layer="narrative">The coverage</p>
        <h2 data-layer="fact">What the coverage got right, and what it got wrong</h2>

        <p data-layer="narrative">The held articles were checked against the enrolled bill, the Clerk's roll call, the Senate roll call, and the White House signing note. Political color (who "engineered" past shutdowns, who "waved the white flag") is the speaker's or the member's record, not a finding of the bill.<Cite s="house-clerk-roll286" passage="Passed" /></p>

        <SourcedBlock source="abc-house-funding" kind="checked">
          <p><strong>ABC</strong> (John Parkinson) wrote that the House passed a continuing resolution through December 11 by 370 to 48, with 19 Republicans and 29 Democrats opposed, and that the Senate had passed it on August 8. Those figures check out against the Clerk and the Senate XML. ABC wrote that the vote sent the measure to the president for signature. On September 1 that was true; it was not yet law.<Cite s="abc-house-funding" passage="By a count of 370 to 48" /><Cite s="house-clerk-roll286" passage="193" /></p></SourcedBlock>

        <SourcedBlock source="ap-house-funding" kind="checked">
          <p><strong>The Associated Press</strong> (Kevin Freking) reported the 370-48 vote, that the Senate had already approved the measure, and Cole's "certainty" line. Those check out. AP, through DeLauro, said the Senate text prevents Homeland Security from transferring funds to the Border Patrol and delays a grants rule. The grants delay is section 157. Murray's "Border Patrol" sentence is her wording; the record behind it is the Homeland Security law this act carries forward, which bars moving money into Customs and Border Protection's border-security operations account.<Cite s="ap-house-funding" passage="The House passed the bill by a vote of 370-48" /><Cite s="plaw-119-86" passage="SEC. 549. Notwithstanding section 503(c) of this Act, no" /></p></SourcedBlock>

        <SourcedBlock source="thehill-house-funding" kind="checked">
          <p><strong>The Hill</strong> reported 370-48, 19 Republicans and 29 Democrats opposed, and a Senate margin of 90-6. Those check out against the Senate's "Bill Passed (90-6, 3/5 majority required)." It attributed opposition among some House Republicans to a hemp-ban delay and the grants rule. The grants rule is in the bill. The hemp characterization is The Hill's; the bill's own heading is "SECTION 781 EXTENSION," and the delay is real, with the lab-made-cannabinoid part not delayed.<Cite s="thehill-house-funding" passage="passed the House on a 370-48 vote" /><Cite s="senate-vote-228" passage="Bill Passed (90-6, 3/5 majority required)" /></p></SourcedBlock>

        <SourcedBlock source="cbs-house-funding" kind="checked">
          <p><strong>CBS</strong> reported a 370 to 48 vote after leaders fast-tracked the bill, a two-thirds majority, Senate approval in early August, and a December 11 expiry. Those check out. Its line that "Funding is now set to expire on Dec. 11" was, on September 1, a description of the bill the House had just passed, not of a signed law.<Cite s="cbs-house-funding" passage="passed the measure in a 370 to 48 vote" /></p></SourcedBlock>

        <SourcedBlock source="nbc-house-funding" kind="checked">
          <p><strong>NBC</strong> reported 370-48, funding through December 11, and that the bill would go to the president, who was expected to sign it. That checks out for a September 1 dispatch. The signature came the next day.<Cite s="nbc-house-funding" passage="sailed through the chamber by a vote of 370-48" /><Cite s="wh-hr6500-signed" passage="the President signed into law:" /></p></SourcedBlock>

        <SourcedBlock source="npr-house-funding" kind="checked">
          <p><strong>NPR</strong> reported 370-48, current levels with a few exceptions through December 11, and that the White House had encouraged the stopgap. The vote and the date check out. The administration's August 3 statement is the White House paper on that encouragement.<Cite s="npr-house-funding" passage="The legislation passed the House 370-48 with bipartisan support" /><Cite s="omb-sap-hr6500" passage="Every Member of" /></p></SourcedBlock>

        <SourcedBlock source="pbs-house-funding" kind="checked">
          <p><strong>PBS NewsHour</strong> ran the Associated Press dispatch. The 370-48 vote, the Cole quote, and the Senate-already-passed line are the same AP copy checked above.<Cite s="pbs-house-funding" passage="The House passed the bill by a vote of 370-48" /></p></SourcedBlock>

        <SourcedBlock source="upi-house-funding" kind="checked">
          <p><strong>UPI</strong> reported 370-48, 29 Democrats and 19 Republicans opposed, funding through December 11, and Senate approval in August. Those check out against the Clerk's party table.<Cite s="upi-house-funding" passage="Twenty-nine Democrats and 19 Republicans voted against it" /></p></SourcedBlock>
      </section>

      <section id="claim-check">
        <p class="sec-kicker" data-layer="narrative">One claim, examined</p>
        <h2 data-layer="fact">Claim checked against the record</h2>

        <SourcedBlock source="abc-house-funding" kind="checked">
          <p><strong>The claim:</strong> the House passed a funding bill, avoiding a shutdown. <strong>The record:</strong> the House, by a two-thirds vote, agreed to Senate amendments to H.R. 6500. That vote did not itself appropriate a dollar. The enrolled bill appropriates at 2026 rates through December 11. The National Archives lists it as Public Law 119-103, H.R. 6500, approved September 2, 2026. The White House recorded the signature that day. "Avoiding a shutdown" is a fair description of the effect after that signature. It is the wrong description of what the September 1 roll call, standing alone, did. Until the signature, the September 30 lapse date had not moved.<Cite s="abc-house-funding" passage="House passes funding bill, avoiding shutdown" /><Cite s="archives-laws-current" passage="119-103" /><Cite s="house-clerk-roll286" passage="On Motion to Suspend the Rules and Concur in the Senate Amendments" /></p></SourcedBlock>

        <p data-layer="narrative">The Clerk's surviving "AGOA Extension Act" label is stale as a title: the House that afternoon adopted a stopgap. It is not wrong about content. The Senate rewrote the bill into a stopgap and kept a two-year extension of the African Growth and Opportunity Act inside it.<Cite s="house-clerk-roll286" passage="AGOA Extension Act" /><Cite s="house-clerk-roll014" passage="AGOA Extension Act" /><Cite s="hr6500-enrolled" passage="SEC. 2008. EXTENSION OF AFRICAN GROWTH AND OPPORTUNITY ACT." /></p>

        <details class="receipt" data-layer="proof"><summary>how the party split was counted</summary>
          <p>From house-roll286.xml totals-by-party: Republican 193 yea, 19 nay, 6 not voting; Democratic 176 yea, 29 nay, 8 not voting; Independent 1 yea. Sum of yeas 193 + 176 + 1 = 370. Sum of nays 19 + 29 = 48. Not voting 6 + 8 = 14. Member rows in that file: 432. The September 1 Congressional Record Daily Digest: "the whole number of the House is 432." Senate vote 228 XML: 90 yeas, 6 nays, 1 present, 3 absent. Archives current-laws row: 119-103 H.R. 6500 Continuing Appropriations and Extensions Act, 2027 Sept. 2, 2026 140 Stat. 987 24.</p>
        </details>
      </section>

      <section id="markets">
        <p class="sec-kicker" data-layer="narrative">September 1 close</p>
        <h2 data-layer="fact">What the markets said</h2>

        <SourcedBlock source="marketscreener-sept1" kind="record">
          <p>Reuters, via MarketScreener, reported that on September 1 the Dow Jones Industrial Average fell 418.97 points, or 0.79 percent, to 52,766.93, the S&amp;P 500 lost 54.67 points, or 0.71 percent, to 7,631.47, and the Nasdaq Composite dropped 271.11 points, or 1.03 percent, to 26,099.77. The wire attributed the move to yields and oil. It does not mention the funding bill.<Cite s="marketscreener-sept1" passage="The Dow Jones Industrial Average fell 418.97 points" /></p></SourcedBlock>

        <p data-layer="proof">Nine saved articles and the White House and OMB records were searched for market, Treasury, stock and ratings language on September 8; none carry a priced reaction. The MarketScreener pin is the priced close. A betting-site summary of Kalshi's October 1 shutdown contract is not the exchange's record, so it is not on this page.</p>
      </section>

      <section id="what-happened-next">
        <p class="sec-kicker" data-layer="narrative">September 2</p>
        <h2 data-layer="fact">What happened next</h2>

        <SourcedBlock source="wh-hr6500-signed" kind="record">
          <p>The White House briefing note dated September 2 says that on Wednesday, September 2, 2026, the president signed H.R. 6500, the Continuing Appropriations and Extensions Act, 2027. The note says the act provides fiscal year 2027 appropriations through December 11, 2026, and extends authorities including surface transportation and veteran programs.<Cite s="wh-hr6500-signed" passage="On Wednesday, September 2, 2026, the President signed into law:" /></p></SourcedBlock>

        <SourcedBlock source="archives-laws-current" kind="record">
          <p>The National Archives current public-laws list records it as 119-103, H.R. 6500, Continuing Appropriations and Extensions Act, 2027, September 2, 2026, 140 Stat. 987, 24 pages.<Cite s="archives-laws-current" passage="119-103" /></p></SourcedBlock>

        <SourcedBlock source="thehill-trump-signs-funding" kind="checked">
          <p>The Hill, writing that night, said the president signed the stopgap into law and restated the 90-6 and 370-48 margins. Those margins match the two roll calls. The Hill's "until at least Dec. 11" does not match section 106. "At least" makes December 11 a minimum. The law makes it the outer date: the money ends at the earlier of a full-year act or December 11.<Cite s="thehill-trump-signs-funding" passage="until at least Dec. 11" /><Cite s="hr6500-enrolled" passage="whichever of the following first occurs" /></p></SourcedBlock>

        <SourcedBlock source="collins-house-passage-2026-09-01" kind="record">
          <p>Collins, after the House vote and before the signature, said she hoped the stopgap would "quickly be signed into law" so work could return to the twelve bills. The White House note is that signature.<Cite s="collins-house-passage-2026-09-01" passage="I hope this CR will quickly be signed into law" /></p></SourcedBlock>

        <SourcedBlock source="house-clerk-roll288" kind="record">
          <p>The same afternoon, after the stopgap, the House agreed to H. Res. 1499, a rule for other bills, {ruleVote.yeas} to {ruleVote.nays}. Republicans voted {ruleVote.republican.yea} to {ruleVote.republican.nay}. Democrats voted {ruleVote.democratic.yea} to {ruleVote.democratic.nay}. Independent Kiley voted yes. The five Republicans who voted no were {ruleVote.republicanNays.join(", ")}. The two Democrats who voted yes were {ruleVote.democraticYeas.join(", ")}.<Cite s="house-clerk-roll288" passage="On Agreeing to the Resolution" /></p></SourcedBlock>

        <p data-layer="narrative">The Guardian and Axios carried a joint statement from Jeffries, Clark, and Aguilar on that rule vote: the two Democrats had "blindside the entire House Democratic Caucus," a "significant breach of trust," and "we have asked the Committee on Caucus Rules to take this up in short order." Axios reported that Golden "defended his vote by noting that the rule also contained a bill he co-authored to shield lobstermen."<Cite s="guardian-house-funding" passage="blindside[d] the entire House Democratic Caucus" /><Cite s="axios-golden" passage="blindside the entire House Democratic Caucus" /><Cite s="axios-golden" passage="defended his vote by noting that the rule also contained a bill" /></p>

        <p data-layer="narrative">Watching: whether any of the twelve full-year 2027 appropriations bills is signed before December 11; whether the Uniform Guidance rewrite or the hemp delay is extended past that date, because both also end then; and whether a further stopgap is needed once the new Congress is organized.<Cite s="delauro-house-passage-2026-09-01" passage="passing all twelve full-year funding bills before the expiration of this resolution" /><Cite s="hr6500-enrolled" passage="through December" /></p>
      </section>

      <section id="unknowns">
        <p class="sec-kicker" data-layer="narrative">Open questions</p>
        <h2 data-layer="fact">What we do not know yet</h2>
        <ul data-layer="fact">
          <li>CBO published an estimate of the Senate text in August; its site blocked our saves on September 8, so its figures are not on this page.<Cite s="hr6500-enrolled" passage="December 11, 2026" /></li>
          <li>No Jeffries statement on the stopgap is in our records. The leadership statement that day was about the rule vote.<Cite s="axios-golden" passage="blindside the entire House Democratic Caucus" /></li>
          <li>Whether any of the twelve full-year 2027 bills will be signed before December 11. Cole said he wants to finish them and does not know if that can be done until after the election. That is his forecast, not a date in the act.<Cite s="nbc-house-funding" passage="I do want to finish all the bills before the end of the year" /></li>
        </ul>
      </section>

